jumanji welcome to the jungle turned out  far better than anyone had any right to  expect what initially sounded soulless  and cynical ended up being a massively  successful well-received family-friendly  thrill ride for kids and adults alike  starring Dwayne Johnson Kevin Hart Jack  Black and Karen Gillan the movie  effectively built on the 1995 story  about a magical board game with a video  game based body swap comedy sequel  welcome to the jungle made a lot out of  a wacky premise but that doesn't mean it  was perfect  many of its quirks won't be picked up by  younger viewers but the adults in the  room probably noticed them here are some  of the things only grown-ups will notice  in Jumanji welcome to the jungle what  console is that Jumanji opens in 1996  introducing viewers to a teenage boy  named Alex in a sleeveless Metallica tee  whose bedroom is covered in pop culture  posters from the era  nothing says 1996 teenager like his  quick dismissal of the Jumanji board  game in favor of his trusty PlayStation  controller we see he's playing twisted  metal a game that was released for the  PlayStation in November 1995 but when we  paused to marvel at all the authentic  90s stuff in his room we get a good look  at the console itself  spoiler alert that's no PlayStation it  doesn't even remotely resemble a  PlayStation from the looks of things  it's closest twin is the Atari 2600 a  wood-paneled console that was already 20  years old in the mid 90s and of course  since you've noticed it once you're not  going to be able to ignore that all the  controllers go from Playstation to  vaguely Nintendo esque when the kids  pull the game out at the school  fittingly enough they can't make heads  or tails of this odd contraption either  there's like some type of old-school  Nintendo something I don't think so  don't recognize it have your cheesecake  it seems to be a common belief that just  acknowledging overdone cliched tropes is  enough to subvert them in movies or  shows but that's not really always the  case Jumanji uses Martha as a mouthpiece  to criticize the unnecessarily skimpy  outfit worn by her avatar Rivi  roundhouse  but as good a sentiment as this might be  the film is making this commentary on  how women are designed in video games  while it simultaneously benefits from  having a young actress in a skimpy  outfit fortunately the movie is brimming  with charm and doesn't rely too heavily  on sexy shots of a woman's body to keep  people watching in fact she has bits of  her male companions outfits to her own  in order to feel a bit more comfortable  as she journeys through the jungle  kicking bad guy butt with her dance  fighting techniques but the marketing  for the movie has no such subtlety  making this a noticeable instance of  Jumanji wanting to have its cake and eat  it too  perish the thought at the midpoint in  the film when the teens have been set  upon by corrupt researcher Russell Van  Pelt and there's no obvious way to move  forward the missing teenager Alex shows  up in the Avatar of Jefferson seaplane  McDonagh he leads our heroes through a  hallway full of authentically videogame  e traps to his little hideaway set apart  from the main Jumanji playing area he's  been holed up there for who knows how  long keeping his head down and trying to  hold on to his last life he explains to  the new kids that the little bungalow  where he lives  drinking delicious margaritas is not a  structure of his own making  instead it once belonged to a man named  Alan Parrish Alan Parrish is of course  Robin Williams character from the  original Jumanji not only as the  reference a lovely tip of the hat to  Alan's character it also gives fans of  the original movie a glimpse at how Alan  was living during his years away Alex is  amazing slang she's pretty fly she mom  I'm just saying I'm sure they'd like to  get jiggy with her Alex is tortured 90  slang is out of place in the jungle and  also this century but his lame-o  vocabulary helps the four kids realize  that the Alex they've found inside the  game is Alex freak the boy who's been  missing and stuck in Jumanji for 20  years it's not a bad idea to use  outdated slang to tip off the characters  that alex is from another era but there  are a few problems here  one is that Will Smith's gettin jiggy  with it came out in 1997 1996 plus  exactly zero people have ever actually  used the phrase gettin jiggy  in conversation ever ask your parents  also if they were going to go there with  that style of slang they probably should  have gone with a different  characterization for Alex instead of a  metallic of tea and a grungy hairstyle  he probably should have been wearing a  cross colors tank top in some radical  prints the walls of his bedroom should  have been covered in TLC and Michael  Jordan posters  now that's 90s instead his lingo comes  off as forced everyone knows mid 90s  metalheads didn't say anything was the  bomb and again no one really knows what  they say sometimes the empty fridge  well Kevin Hart does good work as  fridges avatar the fridge himself has no  real arc and learns no valuable life  lessons in the movie things start off  promising we learn that fridge asked  Spencer to do his homework as a favor  which turns out to be a bad idea  Spencer may be a nerd but he's not  necessarily a good student and the bogus  paper he provides for fridge is easily  sniffed out by his teacher as a result  fridge gets kicked off the football team  later when fridge and Spencers tension  comes to a head on a cliff in Jumanji  they get into a shoving match costing  Spencer one of his lives but by working  together through the rest of the  adventure they gain a mutual respect for  one another still at the end of the  movie the problems that led to that  cliff confrontation are still there the  boys haven't hashed out their personal  conflict fridge doesn't apologize for  using Spencer to do his homework nor for  insulting and intimidating him in  Jumanji they don't team up in the real  world to get fridge reinstated to the  football team there's no shot of fridge  working hard on his own homework or  doing anything to wrap up the problems  that got him in detention in the first  place all that's changed about him at  the end of his bogus journey with  Spencer Bethany and Martha is that he  seems nicer and vaguely content the  movie doesn't want you to notice it but  his academic and athletic future is  still severely compromised by the  movie's end compare him to the other  characters  Bethany finally begins to take an  interest in the world outside herself  Spencer gets the nerve to take a chance  and kiss Martha and Martha gets over her  shy nervous Nissen ufff to kiss him back  frig just been all afternoon playing  video games but all of his problems are  still there that's kind of how real life  works too  the Alex paradox Jumanji is a family  movie so a happy ending is pretty much  par for the course but let's put on our  thinking caps for a second the  implications of Alex returning to the  moment of his disappearance after being  trapped inside the game for 20 years  should probably be more severe than the  movie lets on no seriously let's talk  about this if a butterfly flapping its  wings in one location can have a  dramatic ripple effect on everything  else in the world then a human teenager  going missing would fundamentally change  the fabric of at least Brantford New  Hampshire Alex's unexplained  disappearance from this small town was a  major cultural touchstone for everyone  who lived there it's mentioned multiple  times by different characters at the  start of the film and it's the sort of  event that would have all kinds of small  effects on people's relationships and  behavior parents probably have his  disappearance in the back of their minds  while they're raising their kids old man  freak who is once vigorous and full of  life has his destiny dramatically  changed by the anguish from mysteriously  losing his son the disappearance leads  other people in town to be more cautious  over the years that follow the ending  should have been more like Back to the  Future with the tiniest changes in the  past having major effects in the years  that followed but nope that never  happened  even after the timeline where Spencer  fridge Martha and Bethany were living  and gets winked out of existence and  replaced by this new world where Alex  never went missing everything outside of  Alex's family ends up being exactly the  same  beyond the confines of his household it  appears that Alex's absence from the  world meant just about nothing that's  insane right at no point in your  rambling incoherent response were you  even close to anything that could be  considered a rational thought look okay  fine sure we realized this is a movie  about a magical video game but that  still just doesn't make a whole lot of  sense okay  thanks for watching click the looper  icon to subscribe to our YouTube channel  plus check out all this cool stuff we  know you'll love to
 

